  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Write the proportionality statement (e.g. y ∝ x²)",
      "Convert to an equation: y = kx²",
      "Substitute the known values to find k",
      "Write the full equation with the value of k",
      "Substitute the new value to find the unknown"
    ],
    "title": "How to Handle Proportion with Powers",
    "content": "<p>At higher tier, \\(y\\) may be proportional to a <strong>power</strong> of \\(x\\):</p><p>$$y \\propto x^2 \\Rightarrow y = kx^2 \\qquad y \\propto \\sqrt{x} \\Rightarrow y = k\\sqrt{x} \\qquad y \\propto \\frac{1}{x^2} \\Rightarrow y = \\frac{k}{x^2}$$</p><p>The method is the same: substitute a known pair to find \\(k\\), then use the equation to find unknowns. Always write the proportionality statement first, convert it to an equation with \\(k\\), then solve.</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>\\(y\\) is proportional to \\(x^2\\). When \\(x = 3\\), \\(y = 36\\). Find \\(y\\) when \\(x = 5\\).</strong></p><p>\\(y = kx^2\\). \\(36 = k \\times 9\\), so \\(k = 4\\).</p><p>\\(y = 4 \\times 25 = 100\\).</p>"
  },
